    "evidence_text": "Birth weight and mammographic density among postmenopausal women in Sweden.\n\nResults were analyzed using generalized linear models controlling for possible confounders.\n\nPooled analysis of prospective cohort studies on height, weight, and breast cancer risk.\n\nThe authors found little evidence for interaction with other breast cancer risk factors.\n\nThe association between BMI and breast cancer varies by menopausal status.\n\nWeight control may reduce the risk among postmenopausal women.\n\nDESIGN Cohort study.   \n\n\nIntrauterine environments and breast cancer risk: meta-analysis and systematic review\n\nBirth order, prematurity, and maternal smoking were unrelated to breast cancer risk.   \n\n\nBACKGROUND Emerging evidence suggests an association between female prenatal experience and her subsequent risk of developing breast cancer.\n\nBreast cancer and obesity: an update.\n\nHowever, before menopause increased body weight is inversely related to breast cancer risk.\n\nRole of birthweight in the etiology of breast cancer.\n\nBreast cancer may originate in utero.\n\nPregnancy characteristics and maternal risk of breast cancer.\n\nMAIN OUTCOME MEASURE Incidence of invasive breast cancer.   \n\n\nCONCLUSIONS Placental weight is positively associated with maternal risk of breast cancer.\n\nThese results further support the hypothesis that pregnancy hormones are important modifiers of subsequent maternal breast cancer risk.",
